    "evidence_text": "Innate immunity induced by composition-dependent RIG-I recognition of hepatitis C virus RNA\n\nLoss of DExD/H box RNA helicase LGP2 manifests disparate antiviral responses.\n\nCytosolic viral sensor RIG-I is a 5'-triphosphate-dependent translocase on double-stranded RNA.\n\nThis functional integration of two RNA molecular patterns may provide a means to specifically sense and counteract replicating viruses.\n\nThis dialog may be affected by the individual's genetic background.\n\nPIC increased MDA5 and PTPN2 mRNA expression, which was inhibited by the specific siRNAs.\n\nThese findings indicate that changes in MDA5 and PTPN2 expression modify beta-cell responses to dsRNA.\n\nMDA5 regulates inflammatory signals, whereas PTPN2 may function as a defence mechanism against pro-apoptotic signals generated by dsRNA.\n\nImmune signaling by RIG-I-like receptors.\n\nThe recognition of pathogenic DNA is important to the initiation of antiviral responses.\n\nOur results suggest that DDX41 is an additional DNA sensor that depends on STING to sense pathogenic DNA.\n\nRIG-I-mediated antiviral responses to single-stranded RNA bearing 5'-phosphates.",
